Fix $\alpha>0$, $M=\lfloor\alpha N\rfloor$, and $f\in C_b(\mathbb R)$. Suppose the law of $A_N$ is invariant under left and right orthogonal multiplication and, in probability, $$\label{eq:spectralhyp}
\frac1{r_N}\sum_{r=1}^{r_N}\delta_{s_{N,r}}\Longrightarrow\nu,
\qquad
\max_{r\le r_N}\operatorname{dist}(s_{N,r},\operatorname{supp}\nu)\longrightarrow0,$$ where $\nu$ is a deterministic compactly supported probability measure on $[0,\infty)$. Then $F_N(A_N,f)$ converges in probability and in mean to a deterministic value $F_{\alpha,\nu}(f)$.*


*Here is its formula. Approximate the singular slots in operator norm by finitely many positive values $s_1,\ldots,s_h$ whose multiplicities satisfy $n_j/M\to p_j>0$. Thus $\sum_jp_j=\min(1,1/\alpha)$. Put $v_j=\alpha p_j$, and, if $\alpha<1$, add an input null block with $v_0=1-\alpha$. Let $\mathcal I=\{1,\ldots,h\}$ when $\alpha\ge1$ and $\mathcal I=\{0,1,\ldots,h\}$ otherwise. The variable $\rho_i$ is the fraction of the input squared norm assigned to block $i$. The value for this finite approximation is $$\label{eq:radialformula}
\sup_{\substack{\rho_i>0\\\sum_{i\in\mathcal I}\rho_i=1}}
\left\{
\frac12\sum_{i\in\mathcal I}v_i\log\frac{\rho_i}{v_i}
+\alpha P_p^f\left(\left(\frac{s_j^2\rho_j}{\alpha}\right)_{j=1}^h\right)
\right\}.$$ The limit of <a href="#eq:radialformula" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:radialformula">[eq:radialformula]</a> as the spectral mesh tends to zero is $F_{\alpha,\nu}(f)$. Blocks with zero limiting proportion are not used in <a href="#eq:radialformula" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:radialformula">[eq:radialformula]</a>; the no-outlier assumption ensures the required approximations with positive limiting proportions.

For fixed $0<\theta<1$, fixed column counts $d_j$, and $n_j/M\to p_j>0$, let $D$ be bounded continuous, or the sum of a bounded continuous function and a nonpositive quadratic form. Then $$\label{eq:Gaussianlimit}
\frac1M\log\mathcal Z_{M,\theta}\left(\sum_{r=1}^M D(\xi_r)\right)
\longrightarrow\mathcal G_{\theta,p}(D)$$ in probability and in expectation. More generally, let each $g_\ell$ be bounded continuous or a nonnegative quadratic form, let $\bar g=M^{-1}\sum_r g(\xi_r)$, and fix $d>0$ and $c\in\mathbb R^s$. The pressure for $$H=\sum_rD(\xi_r)-Md\sum_{\ell=1}^s(\bar g_\ell-c_\ell)_+$$ converges in probability to $$\label{eq:softduality}
\min_{t\in[0,d]^s}\left\{t\cdot c+\mathcal G_{\theta,p}(D-t\cdot g)\right\}.$$ No restriction on $\sum_jp_j$ is needed.

There is a minimizing $C=C(b)$ for $\Psi(C,b)-\operatorname{tr}C/2$. It lies in the strict finite domain, its tilted spin satisfies $\mathbb Ess^{\mathsf T}=I$, and the cross covariances $q^*$ of two tilted visits form an eligible path. Moreover $$\label{eq:canonicalduality}
\begin{split}
J(b)&:=\Psi(C(b),b)-\tfrac12\operatorname{tr}(C(b)+b_{k-1})\\
&=S_\theta(q^*)-\frac12\sum_iw_i\operatorname{tr}(q_i^*b_i)
=\inf_q\left\{S_\theta(q)-\frac12\sum_iw_i\operatorname{tr}(q_i b_i)\right\}.
\end{split}$$ For fixed $\theta,d$ and bounded $b_{k-1}$, all such multipliers are bounded uniformly over the grids. The tilted terminal field has bounded second and fourth moments under the same uniformity.

The full-system overlap array has the same GG law as the two-omitted limit. Along its joint matrix-and-label order, the off-diagonal limits of $B_j$ are deterministic bounded nondecreasing positive semidefinite paths $b_j(u)$. Their diagonal is a deterministic matrix $B_{j,\mathrm d}\succeq b_j(u)$. The indicated one-path averages have deterministic limits, including $c_j$, and $$\label{eq:bulkidentity}
c_j+\operatorname{tr}B_{j,\mathrm d}
=\int_0^1\operatorname{tr}(q_j(u)b_j(u))\,du.$$ The paths $b_j$ are functions of the actual joint overlap and label value; there is no extra variation on a flat portion of that value.

Sort $a_j/p_j$ in nonincreasing order. If $b_j\ge0$ satisfy $$\label{eq:prefix}
\sum_jb_j=\sum_ja_j,\qquad
\sum_{j\le i}b_j\ge\sum_{j\le i}a_j\quad(1\le i<h),$$ then $\Phi(b)\le\Phi(a)$.

In Theorem <a href="#thm:main" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:main">2</a>, or Proposition <a href="#prop:aspect" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:aspect">7</a>, let $s_-=\min\operatorname{supp}\nu$ and $s_+=\max\operatorname{supp}\nu$. The no-outlier assumption may be weakened to $$\label{eq:edges}
\max_{r\le\min(M,N)}\operatorname{dist}(s_{N,r},[s_-,s_+])\longrightarrow0
\quad\hbox{in probability}.$$ If $\alpha<1$, the interval may instead be $[0,s_+]$, using the macroscopic structural right null space at its lower endpoint. Forced left zeros do not provide such an endpoint.

For fixed interval weights, uniformly on compact sets of nonnegative nondecreasing step field values,*


*$$\label{eq:spherical-dual}
l_n(h)\ \longrightarrow\ \min_{q_0\le\cdots\le q_{k-1}\,;\ 0\le q_i<1}
       \left\{S(q)-\sum_i w_i h_i q_i\right\}$$*

*as $n\to\infty$. Furthermore, for each fixed $0\le B<1$, if $0\le q(u)\le B$ a.e. is nondecreasing and*


*$$\label{eq:stationary-field}
2h(u)=A_q(q(u)),\qquad A_q(r):=\int_0^r\frac{dt}{D_q(t)^2}\quad(0\le r<1),$$*

*then*


*$$\label{eq:spherical-uniform}
l_n(h)=S(q)-\int_0^1 hq+o(1),$$*

*where the error tends to zero uniformly over these $q$.

Choose any deterministic parameters $v(N)\in\mathcal G_N$, with $N$ running along any subsequence tending to infinity. Suppose that the omitted system’s overlap array and the full system’s arrays $$\big(R_{\ell j},B_N(x^\ell,x^j),C_N(x^\ell)\big)_{\ell,j\ge1}$$ converge in law in all finite restrictions, under their respective disorder and Gibbs averages. Let $\mu$ be the off-diagonal two-replica law of the omitted limiting array, and let $q$ be its quantile. Then the limiting full and omitted overlap arrays have the same law. There are constants $a_{\rm d},c$ and a deterministic nonnegative nondecreasing function $a$, specified $\mu$-almost everywhere, such that the full-system limit satisfies almost surely $$\label{eq:bulk-identification}
\begin{aligned}
 B_{\ell j}&=a(R_{\ell j})\quad(\ell\ne j),\qquad
 B_{\ell\ell}=a_{\rm d},\qquad C_\ell=c,\\
 0&\le a\le a_{\rm d}\le\alpha\|f'\|_\infty^2.
\end{aligned}$$ Moreover, $$\label{eq:self-consistency}
\begin{aligned}
 c+a_{\rm d}&=\int r\,a(r)\,\mu(dr),\\
 a(r)&=A_q(r)=\int_0^r\frac{dt}{D_q(t)^2}
                       \qquad(\mu\text{-a.e. }r),\\
 \sup\operatorname{supp}\mu&\le B_*:=
       \frac{\alpha\|f'\|_\infty^2}{1+\alpha\|f'\|_\infty^2}<1.
\end{aligned}$$ The law $\mu$ and the quantities $a,a_{\rm d},c$ may depend on the chosen subsequential limit; the bound $B_*$ does not.
